The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
entry = find_dequeue_entry(queue, ntohl(vhdr->id), info->net);
...
if (entry->state.pf == PF_BRIDGE) {
    err = nfqa_parse_bridge(entry, nfqa);
    if (err < 0)
        return err;
}
```
Reason:
`find_dequeue_entry()` removes the queue entry from the data structures, transferring ownership to the caller. If `nfqa_parse_bridge()` fails, the function returns immediately without freeing the entry, its associated `sk_buff`, or releasing held references (net_device refcount, net namespace refcount). This causes a resource leak that can exhaust kernel memory.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (entry->state.pf == PF_BRIDGE) {
    err = nfqa_parse_bridge(entry, nfqa);
    if (err < 0) {
        nfqnl_reinject(entry, NF_DROP);
        return err;
    }
}
```
Reason:
The added `nfqnl_reinject(entry, NF_DROP)` before the return ensures the dequeued entry is properly freed with an `NF_DROP` verdict, releasing its `sk_buff` and all held references. This prevents the memory leak on the error path, consistent with other error handling in the file.

